From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: mhi_net: stop refilling, and drain the refill work, once
 the device is down

mhi_ndo_stop() cancels the refill work, but nothing stops a DL completion from
re-arming it afterwards. That window has two consequences, and only the second
is serious.

The harmless one is the buffer. A completion that slips past the stop and queues
one is not lost: mhi_gen_tre() checks ch_state == MHI_CH_STATE_ENABLED and
publishes the TRE while holding mhi_chan->lock, and mhi_unprepare_channel()
takes that same lock to set MHI_CH_STATE_DISABLED before mhi_reset_chan()
reclaims what is queued. Either publication wins the lock and teardown reclaims
the buffer with -ENOTCONN, which mhi_net_dl_callback() frees, or the disable wins
and mhi_queue_skb() refuses it and the callback frees it. Nothing is published
that is not then reclaimed.

The serious one is the work item. If that late completion fails to allocate or to
queue, it schedules rx_refill - and mhi_net_dellink() then unprepares the
channels and calls free_netdev() with the work still pending.
mhi_net_rx_refill_work() reaches mhi_netdev, ndev and mdev through the netdev
private area, so that is a use-after-free rather than a stray skb, and gating the
worker on netif_running() would not help: reading the freed ndev is the problem.

So: gate both refill paths on netif_running() in the completion handler -
__dev_close_many() clears __LINK_STATE_START before calling ndo_stop, so the gate
is already false when ndo_stop's cancellation runs - and drain the work once more
in mhi_net_dellink(), after mhi_unprepare_from_transfer() has disabled the
channels so that no new completion can re-arm it.

The drain after unprepare is final, and it is worth writing down why, because
two reviews in a row proposed adding a lock or an RCU section to this driver to
close a window the MHI core already closes:

 - a data xfer callback is only ever invoked from parse_xfer_event(), reached
   from mhi_process_data_event_ring();
 - that runs as mhi_event->process_event() from mhi_ev_task(), which holds
   spin_lock_bh(&mhi_event->lock) ACROSS the call - data rings get this tasklet,
   while MHI_ER_CTRL rings get mhi_ctrl_ev_task() and mhi_process_ctrl_ev_ring(),
   which invokes no data callback;
 - mhi_unprepare_channel() -> mhi_reset_chan() -> mhi_mark_stale_events() takes
   that same mhi_event->lock, so it waits out a callback already executing;
 - and ch_state is set to MHI_CH_STATE_DISABLED under write_lock_irq() before
   that, while parse_xfer_event() rechecks ch_state != MHI_CH_STATE_ENABLED under
   read_lock_bh() and returns without calling the callback.

So once mhi_unprepare_from_transfer() has returned, no further data callback can
run on these channels, and nothing can re-arm rx_refill behind the cancellation
that follows. A driver-side rwlock would add a lock acquire to every rx and tx
completion for this, and an RCU section would need the handlers reworked to stop
re-reading driver_data; neither buys anything here.

(An earlier version of this very paragraph claimed the opposite, reasoning from
mhi_chan->lock being released before the callback at parse_xfer_event(). That is
true and irrelevant: the serialising lock is the event-ring lock, one level up.)

Still unvalidated: only the allocation/queue failure branch reaches the dangerous
path, so a normal successful-refill test never exercises it. Validating it wants
fault injection on that branch concurrent with close and removal, which has not
been done.

Logically this belongs BEFORE the inline refill that made the window reachable;
it is ordered after it here only because that is where the local patch stack
already stood.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
+++ b/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
@@ -240,7 +240,31 @@
 	 * queue_is_full() test above - is the authoritative check, so losing
 	 * that race against the fallback worker only costs one allocation.
 	 */
-	if (!mhi_queue_is_full(mhi_dev, DMA_FROM_DEVICE)) {
+	/*
+	 * Do not hand buffers back to a device that is going down.
+	 * __dev_close_many() clears __LINK_STATE_START before calling
+	 * ndo_stop, so this is false by the time mhi_ndo_stop() runs its
+	 * cancel_delayed_work_sync() - which is what stops an ordinary
+	 * completion arriving after that cancellation from refilling, inline or
+	 * through the work, behind the teardown's back.
+	 *
+	 * This is a gate, not a barrier: a callback already past it can still
+	 * reach the code below. For the BUFFER that is harmless, and the reason
+	 * is the channel lock rather than the order these functions are called
+	 * in. mhi_gen_tre() checks ch_state == MHI_CH_STATE_ENABLED and
+	 * publishes the TRE while holding mhi_chan->lock, and
+	 * mhi_unprepare_channel() takes the same lock to set
+	 * MHI_CH_STATE_DISABLED before mhi_reset_chan() reclaims what is
+	 * queued. So either publication wins the lock and teardown reclaims the
+	 * buffer with -ENOTCONN, which the error path above frees, or the
+	 * disable wins and mhi_queue_skb() refuses it and we free it here.
+	 * Nothing is published that is not then reclaimed.
+	 *
+	 * The WORK ITEM is a different question and is not solved here - see
+	 * the cancellation in mhi_net_dellink().
+	 */
+	if (netif_running(mhi_netdev->ndev) &&
+	    !mhi_queue_is_full(mhi_dev, DMA_FROM_DEVICE)) {
 		unsigned int size = mhi_netdev->mru ? mhi_netdev->mru :
 					READ_ONCE(mhi_netdev->ndev->mtu);
 		struct sk_buff *nskb = netdev_alloc_skb(mhi_netdev->ndev, size);
@@ -363,6 +387,25 @@
 
 	mhi_unprepare_from_transfer(mhi_dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * Drain the refill work again, now that its producers are gone:
+	 * unregister_netdev() ran ndo_stop (whose own cancellation a later DL
+	 * completion could still have defeated) and
+	 * mhi_unprepare_from_transfer() has disabled the channels, so no new
+	 * completion can arrive to re-arm it. Without this, a completion that
+	 * failed to allocate or queue could leave the work pending over
+	 * free_netdev(), and mhi_net_rx_refill_work() reaches ndev and mdev
+	 * through the netdev private area - a use-after-free, not a stray skb.
+	 *
+	 * Not an airtight drain: mhi_unprepare_channel() sets
+	 * MHI_CH_STATE_DISABLED under mhi_chan->lock and resets the channel,
+	 * which stops new events, but nothing there waits for a callback that
+	 * is already executing. MHI exposes no callback-quiesce point to place
+	 * this after, so a completion still in flight can re-arm the work after
+	 * this returns. The window is narrowed, not closed.
+	 */
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&mhi_netdev->rx_refill);
+
 	kfree_skb(mhi_netdev->skbagg_head);
 
 	free_netdev(ndev);
